nt to play **E1** (edge). The move E1 would capture E2, E3, and E4 (bridging to E5). You would lose control of the E-column entirely.\n    *   *Status: Poor.*\n\n5.  **(E, 7)**: This move flips E5, E6, and F6. It is a "C-square" equivalent (next to edge). It allows the opponent to play F8, which captures E7. While not as disastrous as B4 or E2, it flips 3 discs (increasing your count, which can be bad for mobility) and gives the opponent easy access to the bottom edge.\n    *   *Status: Mediocre.*\n\n6.  **(D, 2)**: This move flips E3 and F4. It places a piece in the inner ring.\n    *   *Opponent Response:* The opponent can play **E1** (edge).\n    *   *Consequence:* E1 would capture D2 (bridging to C3).\n    *   *Net Result:* You gain control of E3 and F4 (central pieces) while the opponent gets E1 and flips D2 back. Unlike the E2 or B4 scenarios, the opponent does not sweep a whole row or column. This is a balanced trade that maintains some central influence for you without immediate disaster.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n(D, 2) is the safest and most strategic option. It avoids the immediate corner loss of (G, 7) and the devastating row/column sweeps associated with (E, 2) and (B, 4).\n\nEvaluation: (D, 2) is the best available move. It flips central pieces E3 and F4, strengthening central influence. While it allows the opponent to take the edge at E1, the damage is limited (only D2 is re-flipped), unlike the severe counters available for (E, 2) or (B, 4). It avoids the strategic error of playing on X-squares like (G, 7) or (B, 7).\n\nReason: It avoids giving the opponent a corner (unlike G, 7) and prevents the opponent from making a multi-disc capture on the edge (unlike B, 4 and E, 2). It balances the board better than E, 7.\n\nAnswer: (D, 2)'}
